# Tailwind CSS — Best Practices

## Configuration and production CSS

- **Content scanning (Tailwind v3+):** Configure the `content` array in `tailwind.config` so unused utilities are tree-shaken from production CSS. In Tailwind v2 this was `purge`; v3+ uses `content` with the same intent.

```javascript
// tailwind.config.js — Tailwind v3+
module.exports = {
  content: [
    './src/**/*.{html,js,jsx,ts,tsx,vue}',
    './public/index.html',
  ],
  darkMode: 'media', // or 'class' | false
  theme: { extend: {} },
  plugins: [],
};
```

- **Single source of truth:** Customize colors, spacing, typography, breakpoints, and shadows in `theme.extend`. Avoid one-off magic values in markup when a token will repeat.
- **`theme()`:** In custom CSS or JS, read values from the theme so refactors propagate everywhere.

## Layout, responsiveness, and design system

- **Mobile-first:** Default styles for the smallest breakpoint; add `sm:`, `md:`, `lg:`, `xl:`, `2xl:` as the layout grows.
- **Design system:** Map brand tokens into `tailwind.config` once; components consume utilities derived from those tokens.
- **Prebuilt UI:** Tailwind UI or internal component libraries speed delivery; customize to match tokens. Document shared components (e.g. Storybook) so class patterns stay consistent.

## Components and class strategy

- **Decompose UI:** Small reusable components with predictable props; avoid copying the same 15-class string across files—extract with `@apply` or a shared partial.
- **Utility-first, then abstract:** Prefer utilities; when a pattern repeats, use a semantic class plus `@apply` (e.g. `.btn-primary`) so updates happen in one place.
- **Custom utilities:** Add recurring non-token patterns via `theme.extend` (spacing, colors, keyframes) or a small plugin when a single class should encode a pattern.
- **Inline styles:** Avoid except for truly dynamic values that cannot be expressed as utilities or CSS variables; utilities cover most static styling needs.

## CSS layering and Tailwind features

- **`@layer`:** Put base resets, component bundles, and custom utilities in `components` / `utilities` layers so specificity stays predictable alongside Tailwind’s layers.
- **Variants:** Use `hover:`, `focus:`, `focus-visible:`, `active:`, `disabled:`, `dark:`, etc. Extend `variants` / `plugins` when product needs custom state selectors.
- **Arbitrary values:** Bracket syntax (`w-[23px]`, `top-[13px]`) is fine for edge cases; overuse makes designs untraceable—promote repeats into `theme.extend`.

## Performance

- Correct **`content`** globs → smallest production CSS; wrong globs → missing styles or bloat.
- **JIT** (default in v3+) keeps dev builds fast and generates only requested utilities.
- **Runtime perf:** Large class lists on one node can hurt readability more than CPU; still prefer splitting markup or using `@apply` for clarity. Optimize images, code-split JS, and virtualize long lists where needed—Tailwind does not replace those.

## Code organization

- **Common layouts:** `src/css/` or `styles/` for entry Tailwind file and global layers; `src/components/` for UI; layout shells (header, nav, footer) as separate components when the app is large enough to warrant it.
- **Naming:** Consistent file names (`button.component.ts`, `header.css`) and predictable folder boundaries.
- **Modules:** Optional `@import` of feature CSS into the main Tailwind entry file; keep imports ordered (base → components → utilities).
- **Tests:** Colocate or mirror `*.spec.ts` / `*.test.tsx` next to components or under `__tests__` per project convention.

## Patterns and anti-patterns

| Prefer | Avoid |
|--------|--------|
| Tokens + utilities | Hardcoded hex/rpx scattered in class strings |
| `content` paths for all template sources | Shipping full prebuilt CSS without scanning |
| `@apply` for stable repeated clusters | Re-implementing Tailwind in bespoke CSS |
| Documented shared components | One-off divergent “almost the same” buttons |

- **State-driven styling:** Toggle classes from component state or a store (Redux, Zustand, signals, etc.); keep the mapping between state and classes explicit and easy to audit.

## Security

- Tailwind does not mitigate **XSS**, **CSRF**, or **injection** by itself. Sanitize or escape HTML; validate input; use HTTPS; implement auth correctly.
- **Class names from user input:** Avoid building class strings directly from unsanitized user data (theoretical class-pollution / unexpected styling). Prefer allowlists when dynamic classes are required.

## Testing

- **Unit:** Components and class-mapping helpers in isolation.
- **Integration:** Feature flows with real DOM or testing-library.
- **E2E:** Critical paths in a browser. Use mocks for network/time where appropriate.

## Pitfalls and debugging

- **Production missing styles:** `content` globs omit new folders or alternate extensions—add them and rebuild.
- **Overrides:** Understand cascade and `@layer` before chaining `!` important; fix order or specificity instead of fighting Tailwind defaults blindly.
- **Debug:** Use devtools to inspect computed styles; verify the generated class exists in the built CSS when something “does nothing.”

## Tooling

- **Editor:** Tailwind CSS IntelliSense for class completion and lint hints.
- **Build:** PostCSS with `tailwindcss` and `autoprefixer` in typical setups.
- **Lint:** `eslint-plugin-tailwindcss` for class sorting and invalid utilities where configured.
- **CI/CD:** Lint, test, and production build on every merge; fail on errors before deploy.
